Sogen: ReadFile and WriteFile on a socket are a receive and a send

afd.sys serves a socket handle's IRP_MJ_READ and IRP_MJ_WRITE as a receive
and a send without flags, so ReadFile and WriteFile work on a socket as on
a file. Sogen's NtReadFile and NtWriteFile knew the console, NUL, named
pipes and files only, and returned STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE for a socket.

A runtime that treats sockets as files does all its socket I/O that way.
SBCL does: an fd-stream over a socket reads and writes it with overlapped
ReadFile and WriteFile, then waits on its own event. Under Sogen the first
write on any socket failed ("Couldn't write to #<FD-STREAM for socket
...>", from quicklisp's HTTP client): SBCL could not talk HTTP at all,
not even to itself over loopback.

A device can now name the io_control request it serves a read or a write
as (io_device::read_write_request), and NtReadFile and NtWriteFile issue
that request as NtDeviceIoControlFile issues one: the event reset, the
completion port, a pending request completing later. AFD names two
requests of its own past every one mswsock sends, and serves them with the
transfer half of its receive and send, which now stand apart from the
AFD_RECV_INFO and AFD_SEND_INFO parsing; a blocking socket pends until
the host socket is ready, a non-blocking one returns
STATUS_DEVICE_NOT_READY, as a receive or send does.

